{"@timestamp":"2026-10-09T00:38:37.717307Z","actor":"agent:transluce-ingest","body":{"basis":"UPSTREAM","cites":["observation_1b6d847b208c4a0b964955ab13946eb9","observation_04d1780e1b394d7e9d9c82b44f9e231f","observation_752e2d30cfa848e398677cfabbf4de9a","observation_1141a158607c4c2eb2aa3edd0b5744f2","observation_9841463154824c7087709a6cf8902fa7","observation_eb1d0fa169474967b5c59bf3275ab960","observation_ce07ff516a0c4c14b17263d64253c595","observation_28374e5047cc4851882e457f8092a8b5"],"note":"Transluce findings tracker. Summary\n#171 documented the Amap fleet's webhook.site dead-drop inboxes and left the AI company as unknown. We checked who created those inboxes and eight others the fleet used on Oct 6–7, read their contents, and looked at what outside parties posted into them. All of the inboxes were created from Tencent Cloud addresses in Hong Kong. Three were created from the exact IPs that swarmcha linked to the fleet on Oct 4. Two contain the sandbox's own test posts carrying the Via: hysandbox-ats header ","property":"reported","subject":"observable_d5974fede0cd4b1ca1d5351ca966f64b","value":"Follow-up to #171. Its inboxes and eight more from Oct 6–7 were created from Tencent Cloud HK; three reuse IPs swarmchasers tied to the fleet, two carry hysandbox-ats self-tests. Outside parties messaged the fleet from Oct 5, and some later runs use the report's vocabulary."},"fingerprint":"6065c6612c32b26f824e06a5734f6800a1029e9ebc1c0ee8b604504cc1959be8","id":"claim_981cac7b9be24e6fa3c2e7ccc9bd1e37","record_kind":"claim","schema":"urn:factum:core:claim:1","tags":{"ai_company":"Other: likely Tencent (Hunyuan), as in swarmcha's report and finding #153","novelty-check":true,"submitter":"Christopher Ta"}}
